{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012hPgqeCXzNv3uug1Ec8GLj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd027-paste\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/paste: Merge Lines of Files Side by Side\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  macOS BSD paste is functionally equivalent to GNU paste for common usage, but there are\n6\t  edge-case differences in delimiter cycling behavior and the treatment of exhausted files.\n7\t  Scripts that merge columnar data from multiple files depend on byte-exact output. The Go\n8\t  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gpaste, installed by\n9\t  coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via\n10\t  pkg/testutils.\n11\t\n12\tgoals:\n13\t  - G1: Read one line from each file in turn and write them as a single output line separated by a delimiter, matching GNU paste behavior.\n14\t  - G2: Support -d to configure the delimiter or delimiter list used between fields.\n15\t  - G3: Support -s to paste one file at a time in serial mode (all lines of one file on one output line).\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Default parallel merge\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: Must open all named files simultaneously and read one line from each per output line. Lines from files are joined with the delimiter and written to stdout, followed by a newline.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: When files have unequal line counts, the shorter files contribute empty fields (the delimiter is still written) until all files are exhausted. The final output line ends with a newline.\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: stdin is used as an input when \"-\" is given as a filename. Multiple \"-\" arguments each refer to stdin read sequentially.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: When no files are given, must read from stdin and write each line followed by a newline (passthrough behavior matching gpaste with a single stdin source).\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Delimiter configuration (-d)\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: -d DELIM must use DELIM as the separator between fields. DELIM may be a single character or a list; if a list is given, the delimiters are cycled in order across the fields of each output line.\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: The escape sequences \\\\n (newline), \\\\t (tab), \\\\\\\\ (backslash), and \\\\0 (empty string, no delimiter) are recognized in DELIM.\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: When DELIM cycles back to the start for a new output line, cycling resets from the first delimiter in the list.\n39\t\n40\t  R3:\n41\t    title: Serial mode (-s)\n42\t    items:\n43\t      - R3.1:\n44\t          text: -s must process files one at a time. All lines of the first file are joined with the delimiter and written as a single output line. Then all lines of the second file are written as a single output line, and so on.\n45\t      - R3.2:\n46\t          text: In serial mode, the delimiter list cycles across fields within the single output line for each file. The trailing newline is always written.\n47\t      - R3.3:\n48\t          text: -s and the default parallel mode are mutually exclusive; -s overrides parallel mode.\n49\t\n50\t  R4:\n51\t    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n52\t    items:\n53\t      - R4.1:\n54\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n55\t      - R4.2:\n56\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing halts on the first error.\n57\t      - R4.3:\n58\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n59\t      - R4.4:\n60\t          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n61\t\n62\tnon_goals:\n63\t  - cmd/paste does not buffer entire files in memory; it reads one line per file per output line in parallel mode.\n64\t  - cmd/paste does not implement Unicode multi-byte delimiter expansion beyond the recognized escape sequences.\n65\t\n66\tacceptance_criteria:\n67\t  - id: AC1\n68\t    criterion: \"paste with two files containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' and '1\\\\n2\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\t1\\\\nb\\\\t2\\\\n', matching gpaste.\"\n69\t    traces:\n70\t      - R1.1\n71\t      - R1.2\n72\t      - R1.3\n73\t      - R1.4\n74\t  - id: AC2\n75\t    criterion: \"paste -d: with the same files produces 'a:1\\\\nb:2\\\\n', matching gpaste -d:.\"\n76\t    traces:\n77\t      - R2.1\n78\t  - id: AC3\n79\t    criterion: \"paste -s with a file containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\tb\\\\tc\\\\n', matching gpaste -s.\"\n80\t    traces:\n81\t      - R3.1\n82\t      - R3.3\n83\t  - id: AC4\n84\t    criterion: \"paste with files of unequal length produces correct output with empty fields for exhausted files, matching gpaste.\"\n85\t    traces:\n86\t      - R1.2\n87\t      - R2.2\n88\t  - id: AC5\n89\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gpaste passes for all required test cases.\"\n90\t    traces:\n91\t      - R1.1\n92\t      - R1.2\n93\t      - R1.3\n94\t      - R1.4\n95\t  - id: AC6\n96\t    criterion: \"cmd/paste compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n97\t    traces:\n98\t      - R1.1\n99\t      - R1.2\n100\t      - R1.3\n101\t      - R1.4\n102\t      - R2.1\n103\t      - R2.2\n104\t      - R2.3\n105\t      - R3.1\n106\t      - R3.2\n107\t      - R3.3\n108\t      - R4.1\n109\t      - R4.2\n110\t      - R4.3\n111\t      - R4.4\n112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e190956e-9a81-464a-b202-945b876410fa","uuid":"35a3f9fa-a6f7-4964-9934-763688b651ab","timestamp":"2026-05-19T20:49:46.413Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5286/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd027-paste.yaml","content":"id: srd027-paste\ntitle: \"cmd/paste: Merge Lines of Files Side by Side\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD paste is functionally equivalent to GNU paste for common usage, but there are\n  edge-case differences in delimiter cycling behavior and the treatment of exhausted files.\n  Scripts that merge columnar data from multiple files depend on byte-exact output. The Go\n  implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gpaste, installed by\n  coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via\n  pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Read one line from each file in turn and write them as a single output line separated by a delimiter, matching GNU paste behavior.\n  - G2: Support -d to configure the delimiter or delimiter list used between fields.\n  - G3: Support -s to paste one file at a time in serial mode (all lines of one file on one output line).\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default parallel merge\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must open all named files simultaneously and read one line from each per output line. Lines from files are joined with the delimiter and written to stdout, followed by a newline.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: When files have unequal line counts, the shorter files contribute empty fields (the delimiter is still written) until all files are exhausted. The final output line ends with a newline.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: stdin is used as an input when \"-\" is given as a filename. Multiple \"-\" arguments each refer to stdin read sequentially.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: When no files are given, must read from stdin and write each line followed by a newline (passthrough behavior matching gpaste with a single stdin source).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Delimiter configuration (-d)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: -d DELIM must use DELIM as the separator between fields. DELIM may be a single character or a list; if a list is given, the delimiters are cycled in order across the fields of each output line.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: The escape sequences \\\\n (newline), \\\\t (tab), \\\\\\\\ (backslash), and \\\\0 (empty string, no delimiter) are recognized in DELIM.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: When DELIM cycles back to the start for a new output line, cycling resets from the first delimiter in the list.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Serial mode (-s)\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: -s must process files one at a time. All lines of the first file are joined with the delimiter and written as a single output line. Then all lines of the second file are written as a single output line, and so on.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: In serial mode, the delimiter list cycles across fields within the single output line for each file. The trailing newline is always written.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: -s and the default parallel mode are mutually exclusive; -s overrides parallel mode.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing halts on the first error.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4:\n          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/paste does not buffer entire files in memory; it reads one line per file per output line in parallel mode.\n  - cmd/paste does not implement Unicode multi-byte delimiter expansion beyond the recognized escape sequences.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"paste with two files containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\n' and '1\\\\n2\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\t1\\\\nb\\\\t2\\\\n', matching gpaste.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"paste -d: with the same files produces 'a:1\\\\nb:2\\\\n', matching gpaste -d:.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"paste -s with a file containing 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' produces 'a\\\\tb\\\\tc\\\\n', matching gpaste -s.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.3\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"paste with files of unequal length produces correct output with empty fields for exhausted files, matching gpaste.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.2\n      - R2.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gpaste passes for all required test cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/paste compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R4.4\n","numLines":112,"startLine":1,"totalLines":112}}}
